res":[{"name":"James Eads","confidence":0.98},{"name":"Andrew Humphreys","confidence":0.98}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Mississippi River","confidence":0.99},{"name":"Yazoo-Mississippi Delta","confidence":0.9}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The compromise river policy developed by James Eads and Andrew Humphreys contributed to the 1927 disaster while enabling Delta cultivation, wealth, aristocracy, and a distinctive culture.","confidence":0.97},"academicOrientation":{"score":32,"confidence":0.86},"profileConfidence":0.96,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Gulf of Mexico","confidence":0.62}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"4752ee46fba12c17c9f6b5b165ae7ee802c1ce25d6c0fcf30e0ee031d1220470","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"In 1927, the Mississippi River swept across an area roughly equal in size to Massachusetts, Connecticut, New Hampshire, and Vermont combined, leaving water as deep as thirty feet on the land stretching from Illinois and Missouri south to the Gulf of Mexico. Close to a million people - in a nation of 120 million - were forced out of their homes. Some estimates place the death toll in the thousands. The Red Cross fed nearly 700,000 refugees for months. Rising Tide is the story of this forgotten event, the greatest natural disaster this country has ever known. But it is not simply a tale of disaster. The flood transformed part of the nation and had a major cultural and political impact on the rest. Rising Tide is an American epic about science, race, honor, politics, and society. Rising Tide begins in the nineteenth century, when the first serious attempts to control the river began. The story focuses on engineers James Eads and Andrew Humphreys, who hated each other. Out of the collision of their personalities and their theories came a compromise river policy that would lead to the disaster of the 1927 flood yet would also allow the cultivation of the Yazoo-Mississippi Delta and create wealth and aristocracy, as well as a whole culture.","publicationYear":1997,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Floods","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Flood control","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Humphreys, A. A. 1810-1883","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Eads, James Buchanan, 1820-1887","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Percy family","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Floods -- Mississippi River Valley -- History -- 20th century","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Flood control -- Mississippi River -- History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-rising-tide-the-great-mississippi-flood-of-1927-and-how-it-changed-america-john-m-barry"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Mississippi River Valley -- History -